    {
      "type": "code",
      "title": "Finding a pair in one pass",
      "lang": "python",
      "code": "def pair_sum(nums, target):\n    seen = set()\n    for n in nums:\n        need = target - n\n        if need in seen:\n            return (need, n)\n        seen.add(n)\n    return None",
      "steps": [
        {
          "narration": "The function takes a list of numbers and a target total, and it promises to find a matching pair.",
          "lines": [
            1,
            2
          ],
          "audioFile": "audio/scene-1-step-0.wav",
          "audioSeconds": 5.88,
          "startFrame": 16,
          "frames": 177
        },
        {
          "narration": "Now the loop walks through the list one number at a time, giving each of them a turn.",
          "lines": [
            3,
            4
          ],
          "audioFile": "audio/scene-1-step-1.wav",
          "audioSeconds": 4.36,
          "startFrame": 199,
          "frames": 131
        },
        {
          "narration": "Here is the heart of the trick: it checks whether that needed partner has been seen before.",
          "lines": [
            5,
            6
          ],
          "audioFile": "audio/scene-1-step-2.wav",
          "audioSeconds": 4.52,
          "startFrame": 336,
          "frames": 136
        },
        {
          "narration": "Otherwise the current number joins the seen set, and the loop simply moves to the next one.",
